NAME
    while, until - execute a loop while or until a condition holds

SYNOPSIS
    while LIST; do LIST; done
    until LIST; do LIST; done

DESCRIPTION
    'while' repeatedly executes its body as long as the condition LIST
    exits 0. 'until' repeatedly executes its body as long as the
    condition LIST exits non-zero.

    break(1) exits the loop immediately; continue(1) jumps to the next
    condition check. A shared 10-second wall-clock safety timeout (also
    enforced across nested $(...) substitutions) aborts runaway loops
    with an error. An interrupt (Ctrl-C or host cancel) terminates the
    loop at the next check, prints '^C', and sets $? to 130.

EXAMPLES
    while false; do echo never; done
    until [ -f /Logs/ready.txt ]; do echo waiting; done

EXIT STATUS
    0   Body never ran, or the last executed body command exited 0.
    N   Exit status of the last body command, or 130 if interrupted.

SEE ALSO
    for(1), if(1), break(1), continue(1), test(1)
